"A Chinese orchestra, called a guoyuetuan in Taiwan, was developed in the twentieth century. It is based on the structure and principles of a Western symphony orchestra, but it uses Chinese instruments. It grew out of the Jiangnan sizhu ensembles in the 1920s. Its instruments are grouped by the way they are played: blown, bowed, plucked, and struck. In Chinese these are 吹, 拉, 彈, and 打.": "50c961974709",
"A Chinese orchestra, called a guoyuetuan in Taiwan, was developed in the twentieth century. It is based on the structure and principles of a Western symphony orchestra, but it uses Chinese instruments. It grew out of the Jiangnan sizhu ensembles in the 1920s. Its instruments are grouped by the way they are played: blown, bowed, plucked, and struck. In Chinese these are 吹, 拉, 彈, and 打. The erhu is a Chinese two-stringed bowed instrument. One end of its small sound box is covered with python skin, and the bow hair passes between the two strings instead of over them. There is no fingerboard, so the player presses the strings with the fingertips in the air. The two strings are commonly tuned a fifth apart. The erhu is not a violin, even though people sometimes call it the Chinese violin. The pipa is a plucked instrument with a pear-shaped body and four strings. Taiwan’s dictionary says a pear-shaped, four-stringed pipa came to the Central Plains in the fourth century from the western regions. The pipa has been played in China for almost two thousand years. In the Chinese word, “pí” is to strike outward with the right hand, and “pá” is to pluck inward. The dizi is a transverse bamboo flute. A bangdi has one blow hole, one membrane hole, and six finger holes. The membrane hole is between the blow hole and the finger holes. It is covered with a thin membrane, the dimo, which makes the sound louder and brighter, with a buzzing quality. The bangdi is shorter and higher, with a clear, bright tone. The qudi is longer and mellower, and about a fourth lower. A drum, a gu, is a skin stretched over a hollow wooden body, played with the hands, sticks, or mallets. A gong, a luo, is a flat bronze disc struck with a mallet. In Chinese opera, the percussion section is particularly important, and a pair of gongs is an essential part of the orchestra. The overtones of a drumhead and of a gong are not whole-number multiples, as you saw in lesson 9. A lot of Chinese music is built on five notes, called gong, shang, jue, zhi, and yu. Any of the five can be the tonic. The guqin was said to have had five strings for these five notes, and the guzheng is traditionally arranged in a pentatonic scale. But this is a tendency and not a rule: some pieces use seven notes. In Taiwan, two old traditions are nanguan, with a pipa held across the lap, and beiguan, with suona, drums, and gongs.": "5c4b54e3d142",
"A drum, a gu, is a skin stretched over a hollow wooden body, played with the hands, sticks, or mallets. A gong, a luo, is a flat bronze disc struck with a mallet. In Chinese opera, the percussion section is particularly important, and a pair of gongs is an essential part of the orchestra. The overtones of a drumhead and of a gong are not whole-number multiples, as you saw in lesson 9.": "5bdf61648905",
